<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Chokwe chiefdoms</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1920 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hunters, traders and artists of the upper Kasai whose 19th-century expansion overran the Lunda Empire and whose sculpture is world-famous.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chokwe">Chokwe</a> lived as hunters and farmers in the highlands of north-eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Angola</a>, organised in chiefdoms whose rulers held titles derived from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire</a>. Their traditions link the chiefly line to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> nobles who left the Lunda court around the 17th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chokwe">Chokwe</a> chiefs ruled small, autonomous territories rather than a single kingdom. In the 19th century the Chokwe became leading traders in beeswax, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and later wild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, buying guns and absorbing captive women and dependants, which drove rapid population growth and expansion. Their carvers produced celebrated masks such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwana-pwo">Mwana Pwo</a> and figures of the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chibinda-ilunga">Chibinda Ilunga</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1887 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chokwe">Chokwe</a> forces sacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> capital and briefly dominated the region. The collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> trade and Portuguese and Belgian conquest brought the chiefdoms under colonial rule by about 1920. The Chokwe are now a major people of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Congo</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1600 CE – 1920 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.54%;width:6.37%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1920 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Chokwe religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chokwe</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a>, <a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, <a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tshokwe, Cokwe, Quioco</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1760&amp;focus=chokwe-chiefdoms" aria-label="Chokwe chiefdoms on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5174 2632 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chokwe chiefdoms lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5662 2807h0M5366 2764h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5494 2888h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5494 2888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Angola. <a href="/atlas?year=1760&amp;focus=chokwe-chiefdoms">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
